=== PROPOSAL ===
  action      : rollback_image (rollback)
  target      : ad
  expect      : Redeploying ad at the pre-hotfix image reference it ran before the 20:52:55 update should bring a serving process back. Observable as: (1) the `{service="ad"}` Loki stream resuming with JVM startup / gRPC bind-and-listen lines followed by the routine 'ad request received' traffic lines that stopped at 20:52:40 — the stream has been completely silent since 20:52:54; (2) frontend traces to ad GetAds acquiring a server-side child span from ad instead of terminating as a childless ~1ms leaf, and that error span class disappearing from sampled frontend traces; (3) frontend's per-dependency ad call error count returning to zero. The first of these is the primary check: if ad logs never resume, nothing else matters.
  within      : 300s
  risk        : If the real failure is pod/container-state rather than artifact choice — ImagePullBackOff, a resource-starved host, or a never-scheduled workload — this recreate spends the outage window on a no-op and delays the correct diagnosis; no pod or container-state evidence was gathered at all, which is the weakest point in this proposal. If the hotfix tag was shipped to fix a real ad-service defect, reverting reintroduces that defect once ad is serving again. And because platform-automation has been flapping this tag all day, a manual rollback may contend with the controller and produce a further restart cycle on ad, turning a static outage into an oscillating one.
  blast radius: ad only for the recreate itself. frontend is ad's inbound caller in this graph and is already seeing hard connection-level refusals on GetAds, so it absorbs no new harm from the recreate window; frontend-proxy and load-generator see it transitively through frontend. cart, checkout, product-catalog, product-reviews and recommendation have no edge to ad in the sampled traces and should see nothing — if they do change state when ad is recreated, that itself is evidence the dependency picture in this brief is incomplete.
  if wrong    : Falsified if, five minutes after the recreate, the ad log stream is still empty of any startup/bind/listen line — that means the container is not starting for a reason independent of which tag is referenced (never scheduled, host resource exhaustion, registry unreachable for both tags), and the artifact-choice diagnosis is wrong. Also falsified if ad comes up and serves but the incident's alerts across the other seven services persist: the brief's own open questions flag that a single frontend→ad error path does not account for an 8-service blast radius, nor for the ~5 minute gap between the 20:52:54 shutdown and the 20:58:01 onset, so there may be a second, unobserved mechanism this action does not touch. A third falsifier specific to this evidence: the change log shows five applies and four reverts of this same tag by platform-automation over ~10 hours, so if the hotfix tag reappears on ad within minutes of the rollback, the controller is reconciling against us and the fix will not hold regardless of whether it was correct.
  rests on    : tr_451a0f2a493a, tr_ee1f8a8e0866, tr_a84d740b0da4
  execution   : not executed - a proposal is a claim, never the change (ADR-0028 §1)

